      "content": "I'm glad I could bring a bit of goblin chaos into your day. Now, let's get into the science behind tempering.\n\nThe temperature range I mentioned earlier, 105°F (40°C) to 82°F (28°C), is crucial because it allows the cocoa butter crystals to form in a specific way. Cocoa butter is a complex mixture of fatty acids and triglycerides, which can exist in different crystal forms.\n\nAt temperatures above 105°F (40°C), the cocoa butter is in a \"liquid crystal\" state, where the molecules are still relatively free to move. This makes it difficult to form stable crystals, leading to blooming.\n\nWhen you heat the chocolate to around 105°F (40°C), you're essentially \"melting\" the existing crystals, allowing you to re-form new crystals in a more stable arrangement. This is known as \"seeding\" the chocolate.\n\nAs you cool the chocolate to around 82°F (28°C), the new crystals start to form, but they need to be in a specific shape and size to create a stable crystal structure. This is where the magic happens – the crystals start to \"stack\" on top of each other in a way that creates a smooth, glossy",
